WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.160
I have here a pipe of sorts. I blow in

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.080
air here and air kinda comes out through this spout.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.680
Just so that you are convinced the air is coming out, watch.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.140
The air is coming out, okay.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:21.280
Now, I'm going to put this very light

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.000
ball just above the spout, okay?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.820
And blow on it.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:42.080
The upward streaming air is supporting the weight of the ball, so that it's in levitation, it's floating in the middle of the air.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.280
So far so good. But now,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:04.180
I can support it completely off the vertical.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.360
Let's see if I can do it in the other direction too.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.620
Did you see that while it's off the vertical the ball is spinning?

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:43.709
This phenomenon has to do with all sorts of questions, a whole range of questions that are often discussed in popular literature, and technical literature,

00:01:43.709 --> 00:01:48.060
and, has to do with, for example, how a plane flies.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.440
Now, the usual explanation is that it's Bernoulli's theorem.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:59.040
That's the technical term that people always invoke. And it's supposed to say that if you have a flow of fluid;

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.220
air, water what have you.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.720
The sum of

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:10.280
density times velocity squared over two, so speed squared plus the pressure, is supposed to be constant.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:17.260
So, if there's a large speed, there's small pressure. And if there's a large pressure there's small speed.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.800
And they'll say, they'll have you believe, that well

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.229
there's fast airflow on this side,

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:28.859
and there's slow airflow on this side. So the fast side has low pressure and the slow side has high pressure.

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:33.359
So the high pressure region is pushing the low pressure region, and so it's going to

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.240
be lifted in this direction, fighting the gravitational force.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.320
And they will say that that's the same thing why the airplane's flying.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:47.920
Now this is not quite right, because Bernoulli's theorem, if you read the actual proof and the statement and so on,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:54.140
says that for each stream line, that is, the flow line of say fluid particles, that's true.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.600
But, you cannot apply it automatically to different stream lines.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.149
And in this case, there is a stream line coming above the ball and there's a stream line below the ball.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.329
But these are different stream lines, you can't apply Bernoulli to both at the same time.

00:03:06.329 --> 00:03:10.919
You can't say, "well, this stream line and this stream line, they have the same kind of constant".

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.019
No, it's not implied in the theorem.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.090
There is another version of Bernoulli, which is called a time-dependent Bernoulli, for potential flow.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:23.759
Which means that if there is no vortex in the system you can apply Bernoulli to the entire fluid region.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.170
But, in this case there's a lot of vortex, you saw the whole thing's spinning. So you cannot apply Bernoulli there either.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.560
So, it's actually a non

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.080
application. You can't use Bernoulli to explain this.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.180
So, what is happening, is the question. What is really

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:53.369
happening has to do with something that is subtle but is in some sense even more primitive than Bernoulli.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.890
You see when we do this:

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:10.529
air is streaming above the ball and is being deflected along the ball like this.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.949
Why does it deflect? Because the air has to stick to the ball.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.799
And in a fluid, it's a continuum, it doesn't want to, sort of, snap out and create a vacuum.

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:26.849
So if there's a solid boundary, for example, some body like this, it wants to go along the boundary of this thing.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.189
And that's why it gets deflected. And underneath it kind of goes like that also. So the long and short of it is

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:46.739
that the whole air flow comes in like this, but goes out like this. So it curved.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:58.220
But whenever the trajectory of a moving object curves, for example, in fluid curves, there must be a force that's curving it. You know? And that force is being supplied by

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.700
the ball.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:05.940
Because ball is leading the fluid around this. Because the fluid wants to cohere, that's the technical term,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.040
and wants to go around the ball.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.480
Well action and reaction. If nature has produced one effect,

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:13.680
it has to pay for it by having a countereffect;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.280
and the reaction is from the fluid, the surrounding fluid, the air, to the ball.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:26.200
And if the ball has supplied this kind of force, in order to deflect the airflow, the air has to supply the counter-force in order

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.850
to lift the ball.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.850
So whenever there is

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.510
a curvature of the flow line

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:39.080
that creates what we call the pressure gradient. If it's curving like this, the inner part has high pressure and outer part has low pressure.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.740
And, mathematically speaking, it's really related with centrifugal force.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:49.980
So centrifugal force for speed v, is like v squared over r, r is the sort of radius of this curvature,

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:53.920
and that's actually the pressure gradient that you are creating.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:06:00.310
So the, again, long and short of it is because you have a high pressure region low pressure region, it's going to be lifted upward.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.130
The ball is providing this

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.769
deflecting, a curving trajectory.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.900
So the ambient fluid

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.400
has to apply a force which compensates for that, which is in this direction.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.160
And this is really an effect of a curving trajectory of any kind.

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:23.589
So if you have a curving trajectory, for example

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:28.030
the radius of this curvature is r, and it's going at the speed v, and it's going around;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.440
it turns out that there is a centrifugal force which is like this. And let's say rho is the density.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:40.900
And this is the pressure gradient, which increases as you go from in to out. That's what the minus sign is.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:44.040
(Brady: So Professor the, the, the ball-)

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:44.980
Yes?

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:50.560
(Brady: -turned the air around the corner? Like it made the, it made the air change direction?)

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:51.060
Yes.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.400
(Brady: So the air gets its revenge in a way, doesn't it?)
- Yes, that's right.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.900
(Well if you're gonna do that then I'm gonna push you the opposite direction.)

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:57.680
That's a very good language!

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.680
(But for the air to get its revenge it has to touch the ball. Like it has to be able to punch it.)

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.340
(So where is the air punching the ball?)

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.047
(Is it from underneath or-?)

00:07:07.047 --> 00:07:07.547
Yes.

00:07:07.547 --> 00:07:10.940
(Because it can't pull it, can it? Because it hasn't got like hooks or anything that can pull with?)

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:16.460
Well, it can pull because, you see, the - if you have air next to the ball and air leaves the ball;

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.690
well, if it can just freely leave the ball

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:24.700
it's going to leave a vacuum behind. But the fact of the matter is the fluid next to the solid;

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.530
it doesn't want to create a vacuum.

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:32.559
It doesn't want a cavity. It wants to sort of cohere. It wants to fill the whole space with some substance.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.810
So, when it's pulling away, by that very effect it's actually pulling the solid as well.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:42.549
And, on the molecular level, it's clear what's happening. You know, molecules are all attracting each other.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:48.730
All the air molecules are kind of connected by springs to neighbouring regions. And when you pull the spring,

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:55.600
that spring, you know, shrinks and then pulls the whole thing in. And similarly if you push the spring it gets compressed and that's what the pressure is.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.380
(Brady: So Professor, as a mathematician and a physicist,)

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.560
(when you're sitting on a plane flying to Japan and you look out the window and you see the wing-)

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:04.060
Yeah?

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:09.800
(-in your head are you imagining the wing being pulled up by the air or pushed up by the air?)

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:14.200
Well, I think it's both. You see the wing is so designed that,

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:19.980
roughly speaking, the air comes in like thi-, sorry. Air comes in like this and goes out like that.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:28.460
So it's current is, the trajectory is deflected. And that creates two effects, both which are conspiring of course, that the air underneath is

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.580
pushing upward and the air above is pulling.

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:37.939
That's a figurative way of speaking but essentially, again, what's going on is that there is a pressure difference.

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:41.719
And you have an effect from high pressure region to low pressure region,

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:49.099
you are pushing against each other. By the way, pressure is, everyone knows, is force divided by area. If you multiply top and bottom,

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:56.620
numerator and denominator, by length, you get force times length, that's called work, that's a form of energy. And area times length, that's volume.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:58.980
So it's actually energy per volume.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.660
Pressure is like potential energy per volume,

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:09.860
and we're saying that if you have a high pressure region, that's high potential energy region, and low potential energy region which is low pressure region,

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:13.520
then the stuff moves - is pushed from high pressure to low pressure.

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.929
Which is exactly the same thing as if you have a, say, a slope like this;

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:26.330
a hill and a valley, where all the balls wants to roll from high potential region to the low potential region. Just rolls down.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:31.309
And it gains some kinetic energy by doing this. So the pressure is acting like the potential energy.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:37.070
Let's, let's do the spoon, actually. So let's go. Yeah, let's do the spoon because I like your, um,

00:09:37.589 --> 00:09:39.589
language of the taking revenge.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:41.720
That's great, that's wonderful.

00:09:43.509 --> 00:09:49.469
So, you can do another home experiment to see that whenever a fluid flow

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:55.500
curves, the trajectory of the fluid curves, it creates a pressure gradient.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:58.020
Difference in pressure, which sucks

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.020
something in on one side and pushes something out on the other side.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:04.100
As soon as the spoon touches the water, the water gets

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:11.320
deflected, you see? From straight vertical down, to the side. And that has the effect of

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.320
pulling the spoon in. You see that? Poof!

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:23.060
And that's because, if the water momentum is changed from this to this, that means that somebody must be

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:28.470
providing the force from left to right and the counter force is applied to the spoon

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:32.459
from right to left and that sucks in the spoon from this side.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:37.720
And if I touch the spoon - the water with the spoon from this side, it's the opposite effect.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:59.000
(Brady: So you can feel that spoon being pulled like a wing?)

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:06.740
Yeah. And that's essentially like the ball, but the picture is seen sideways. You see the

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:13.760
airflow was coming in like this and going out like this. And if you turn it by 90 degrees, it's exactly the same picture.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:22.880
And this is the ball, which is sucked in the direction opposite to the direction of deviation of the fluid flow.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:30.080
[Preview] You see, there's a cup here. And the challenge is to just tuck the ball up and put it in a cup. Now,

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.889
that's of course very, very difficult to do so you shouldn't do this in public,

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.410
because the chances of success are very small.

